  "query_preview": "..\n(This afternoon through next Friday)\nIssued at 1130 AM CST Sat Nov 14 2026\n\nBroad surface high pressure will remain established and gradually\nstrengthen through midweek. High temperatures will moderate\nslightly each day as the continental polar air mass weakens.\nMorning lows will continue in the 30s, maintaining frost and\nfreeze concerns primarily east of the Mississippi River. NBM\ndewpoints were lowered across the area, as model guidance remains\nbiased too high.\n\nAloft, an anomalous upper-level ridge will build into the OH/TN\nValleys through midweek. A weakening West Coast trough will cut\noff from the mean flow Sunday, with the cutoff low tracking SE\ntoward the Gulf Coast. Given a lack of deep-layer moisture,\nexpect little more than mid/upper cloud cover through Monday.\n\nThe upper ridge will continue to amplify late week, pushing max\ntemps 10-15 degrees above normal (highs in the low to mid 70s).\nLow-level moisture return remains limited until next weekend,\nkeeping dry and unseasonably warm conditions across the Mid-\nSouth. Fire danger will creep a little higher each day, as\nminimum relative humidity values potentially fall into the upper\nteens and lower 20s. Additionally, drought conditions will\npersist or worsen with 7-day QPF remaining near zero. LREF\nensemble guidance hints at weak troughing late next weekend,\nthough overall flow remains progressive/slow. Above-normal temps\nlook to persist beyond Day 7.",
